'''Waterzooi''' ({{IPA|nl|ˈʋaːtərˌzoːi|lang|Nl-waterzooi.ogg}}) is a [[soup]] from [[Belgian cuisine|Belgium]] and originating in [[Flanders]]. The second part of the name derives from the [[Middle Dutch]] terms ''sode'', ''zo(o)de ''and ''soot'', words referring to the act of boiling or the ingredients being boiled.<ref name="vdew">''van Dale Etymologisch Woordenboek'', p.965, dr. P.A.F. van Veen & drs. Nicoline van der Sijs, 1989, {{ISBN|90-6648-312-1}}</ref> It is sometimes called ''Gentse Waterzooi'' which refers to the Belgian town of [[Ghent]] where it originated. The original dish is often made of [[Fish as food|fish]], either freshwater or salt, (known as ''Viszooitje''), though today [[Chicken (food)|chicken]] ''waterzooi'' (''Kippenwaterzooi'') is more common. The most accepted theory is that rivers around Ghent became too polluted and the fish there disappeared.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.gentblogt.be/2006/11/17/gentse-waterzooi|title=Gentse Waterzooi » Gentblogt|work=gentblogt.be}}</ref> [[Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor]] ate the rich dish, even after suffering from [[gout]].<ref>Patricia Carson, ''Fair Face of Flanders'' (Belgian Art Research Institute, 2001), p. 124.</ref>
